[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. The reports merged into it describe three
different problems. The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one bug. Each problem below now has its own ticket, with its own count.
</Callout>

## The three problems

<CauseTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first, and these tenants write the day
  first. A booking for 7 April goes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 describes.
  [Issue 1](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/1).
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first
  tenants type what it says, and the form then reads the date day first. Same swap, different code
  path: the parser fix does not reach it. [Issue 2](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/2).
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not a day-month swap. The invite arrives before the
  booking; a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday while one in Toronto gets Monday. That is time zones or
  day boundaries, not the locale. The desk noted these reports in `desk-notes.json`; the table files
  them under `parser` by tenant and counts them there, in the note under it.
  [Issue 3](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/3).

## Where each one stands

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October:

| cause | outcome | ticket |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | [issue 1](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/1), closed |
| `hint` | won't fix: booking-v3 form retired | [issue 2](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/2), closed |
| one-day move | not in the update; the dd/mm fix does not change a one-day move | [issue 3](https://github.com/19Sean96/what-it-shows/issues/3), **open** |

<Callout kind="warn">
Closing #4471 did not close the one-day problem. Keep issue 3 open until engineering looks at it.
</Callout>

## How a report is placed

`groupByCause` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` decides, from the tenant on file:

1. a report from a day-first tenant that came through a booking-v3 form is `hint`;
2. any other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`.

The desk's notes (`desk-notes.json`) then mark the reports it read as a one-day move. They stay
counted under `parser`, so issue 1 covers them too, and issue 3 covers them on their own.

The intake tag does not decide it. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the "tagged as"
column shows how often that guess is wrong. The ticket export also repeats some reports; each id
counts once.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match.
All three problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so
subject matching put them in one ticket. Its consequences list the opposite risk, the same bug
under different subjects; it does not cover different bugs under one subject. A new decision record
should replace it.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- A count is only as good as the merge rule behind it. Read a sample of a big ticket before you
  mark it known.
- The desk's own notes held the third problem. Put what you notice into the logic, not a footnote.

